# CUPS -- the printing system gnome-control-center's Printers panel talks to,
# and a HARD requirement of gnome-control-center rather than an optional extra.
#
# gnome-control-center-48.4/meson.build:235-236 declares cups `required: false`
# and then asserts `cups_dep.found()` on the very next line, then asserts
# cc.has_header() for both cups/cups.h and cups/ppd.h. The soft lookup is
# cancelled twice over, so there is no gnome-control-center in this tree without
# this package AND its development headers.
#
# BLFS 12.4, pinned here rather than in versions.env: versions.env is
# regenerated wholesale from the LFS book by tools/pin-versions.sh, so a desktop
# pin placed there is destroyed by the next `make pin`.
SRC_URL=https://github.com/OpenPrinting/cups/releases/download/v2.4.12/cups-2.4.12-source.tar.gz
SRC_SHA256=b1dde191a4ae2760c47220c82ca6155a28c382701e6c1a0159d1054990231d59
SRC_DIR=cups-2.4.12

# ===========================================================================
# FOUR OF THESE FLAGS EXIST BECAUSE CUPS DECIDES THINGS BY LOOKING AT THE
# BUILD MACHINE. That is the single most important fact about this recipe, and
# none of the four announces itself: each produces a complete, installable cups
# with a different identity or a missing configuration file.
#
# config-scripts/ contains, in full:
#
#   CUPS_USER           greps /etc/passwd for the first of
#                       "lp lpd guest daemon nobody"     (cups-defaults.m4:217)
#   CUPS_SYSTEM_GROUPS  greps /etc/group and collects EVERY match of
#                       "lpadmin sys system root wheel"  (cups-defaults.m4:285)
#   PAMDIR / PAMFILE    first existing of /private/etc/pam.d, /etc/pam.d, then
#                       the template chosen by testing for common-auth,
#                       password-auth, system-auth in turn    (cups-pam.m4)
#   rcdir               first existing of /sbin/init.d, /etc/init.d, else
#                       /etc/rc.d                        (cups-startup.m4:145)
#
# MEASURED, NOT PREDICTED, inside a container seeded with the published package
# set the way .github/workflows/build-level.yml seeds one -- because the first
# reading was taken against a BARE duct/chroot and got two of the four wrong.
# The measurement environment is part of the measurement:
#
#   /etc/pam.d        EXISTS, with system-auth, system-account, login, su
#                     (linux-pam's own package ships them)
#   /etc/passwd       of the candidate list, ONLY nobody
#   /etc/group        of the candidate list, ONLY root
#   /etc/dbus-1       EXISTS
#   init directories  NONE of the three
#   libsystemd.pc     EXISTS (elogind's alias).  systemd.pc  DOES NOT.
#
# --with-cups-user=lp --with-cups-group=lp
#       THE PROBE LANDS ON `nobody` HERE, and the reason is a gap between two
#       correct halves of this same change. duct-filesystem gains the lp user
#       in its passwd TEMPLATE, and images/scripts/seed-etc.sh copies that into
#       /etc during the IMAGE build -- so the builder image has no lp user until
#       it is next rebuilt, and an unflagged cups compiles `nobody` into cupsd
#       in the very pull request that adds the user it failed to find.
#       configure line 12105 writes `#define CUPS_DEFAULT_USER "$CUPS_USER"`
#       and scheduler/conf.c:662 does getpwnam(CUPS_DEFAULT_USER), so this is a
#       string in the binary rather than a line in a file. Neither half is wrong
#       alone; nothing in either points at the other. The flag collapses the gap.
#
# --with-system-groups=lpadmin
#       Same mechanism, security consequence rather than functional. The probe
#       collects EVERY match and only `root` is present, so the unflagged build
#       writes `SystemGroup root` into cups-files.conf -- printer administration
#       granted to root by accident of the image rather than to a group created
#       for it.
#
# --with-ondemand=no
#       ELOGIND'S libsystemd.pc ALIAS AGAIN, and this is the fourth package in
#       the tree to hit it. The Linux default is "yes", which runs
#       `$PKGCONFIG --exists libsystemd` (cups-startup.m4:70) -- and that
#       SUCCEEDS here, because elogind ships the alias so GNOME components
#       resolve. cups then defines HAVE_SYSTEMD and HAVE_ONDEMAND and asks
#       `--variable=systemdsystemunitdir systemd` for a directory to install
#       into -- and elogind provides libelogind.pc and the alias but NOT
#       systemd.pc, so SYSTEMD_DIR comes back EMPTY while cups believes it has
#       socket activation. Fails open. Measured both halves above.
#
# --with-rcdir=no
#       Unset, rcdir probes the build machine and falls through to /etc/rc.d,
#       installing SysV init scripts wherever it lands. `no` is supported
#       directly -- cups-startup.m4:196 guards the INITDIR assignment with
#       `test "x$rcdir" != xno` -- and installs nothing.
#       NOT BLFS's --with-rcdir=/tmp/cupsinit, which dumps the scripts somewhere
#       harmless on a live system and would be WRONG here: this install is
#       DESTDIR-staged, so that would package a /tmp/cupsinit directory.
#
# --with-pam-module IS DELIBERATELY NOT PASSED, and that inverts the instinct
#       every other flag here follows. Reading the AS_IF chain in order:
#
#           AS_IF([test "x$with_pam_module" != x], [ PAMMOD=pam_${...}.so ]
#           ,   [test -f /etc/pam.d/common-auth],   [ PAMFILE="pam.common"   ]
#           ,   [test -f /etc/pam.d/password-auth], [ PAMFILE="pam.password" ]
#           ,   [test -f /etc/pam.d/system-auth],   [ PAMFILE="pam.system"   ]
#           ,   [ ...hunt for pam_unix.so under /lib/security... ])
#
#       setting it takes the FIRST branch and leaves PAMFILE at its default
#       `pam.std`, a generic template naming a module directly. Leaving it unset
#       lets the chain reach the third branch, where /etc/pam.d/system-auth
#       EXISTS because linux-pam's package ships it, and PAMFILE becomes
#       `pam.system` -- the template that defers to THIS TREE'S OWN pam stack.
#       So the flag that looks like it pins an ambient value would replace a
#       correct answer with a generic one. The rule is not "always pass the
#       flag": it is pin the outcome you verified, which here means deliberately
#       not passing one, and asserting the installed /etc/pam.d/cups mentions
#       system-auth rather than merely existing.
# ===========================================================================
#
# The rest, each with what it buys or costs:
#
#   --with-tls=openssl    openssl AND gnutls are both packaged here, and
#                         cups-tls.m4 tries openssl first -- so the default
#                         lands on openssl by FIRST MATCH rather than by
#                         decision, which is the same shape as the libsystemd
#                         alias. Named to state the outcome. The constraint in
#                         TAPEBUILD.toml is BOUNDED for the reason recorded
#                         there.
#   --with-dnssd=no       the default tries mDNSResponder then Avahi; neither is
#                         packaged, so it would fail soft to no DNS-SD.
#                         Printer discovery on the local network is a real
#                         feature and this is a real gap -- recorded as a gap
#                         rather than left silent, because "cups is packaged"
#                         should not be read as covering it. It needs Avahi.
#   --enable-dbus --with-dbusdir=/etc/dbus-1
#                         cupsd's D-Bus notifier is what tells the Printers
#                         panel that a job or a printer changed state. The
#                         directory is stated even though the probe finds it,
#                         because cups only sets it `AS_IF([test -d /etc/dbus-1])`
#                         and an image without that directory would install no
#                         policy file at all -- leaving a cupsd that cannot
#                         claim its bus name and a panel that sees nothing.
#   --enable-libusb       USB printing, which for a desktop is most printing.
#                         libusb is packaged so the default would find it;
#                         naming it means a rebuild in an image without libusb
#                         FAILS rather than silently dropping USB printers.
#   --enable-pam          linux-pam is packaged and PAM is how cupsd
#                         authenticates an administrator. Stated rather than
#                         autodetected for the same reason.
#   --enable-acl          POSIX ACLs on spool files. cups-common.m4:314 is
#                         `test "x$enable_acl" != xno` -- on unless disabled --
#                         followed by AC_SEARCH_LIBS([acl_init], [acl]), and acl
#                         IS packaged here, so the default resolved it by
#                         ambient detection. FOUND BY THE DT_NEEDED AUDIT ON
#                         cupsd, not by reading this file: it appears on no BLFS
#                         page and in no dependency list. Kept and stated.
#   --disable-gssapi      Kerberos, and already opt-in
#                         (`test x$enable_gssapi = xyes`, cups-gssapi.m4:20).
#                         Stated so a reader does not have to go and check.
#   --with-rundir=/run/cups
#                         the default is $localstatedir/run/cups, and
#                         duct-filesystem ships /var/run as a symlink to ../run.
#                         Pointing at the real location keeps this package from
#                         shipping a real directory underneath another
#                         package's symlink.
#   --disable-static      as everywhere else in this tree.
#   --with-docdir         under /usr/share/doc, which finish_install deletes.
CONFIGURE_ARGS="--libdir=/usr/lib \
--disable-static \
--with-tls=openssl \
--with-dnssd=no \
--with-ondemand=no \
--with-rcdir=no \
--with-rundir=/run/cups \
--with-cups-user=lp \
--with-cups-group=lp \
--with-system-groups=lpadmin \
--enable-pam \
--enable-dbus \
--with-dbusdir=/etc/dbus-1 \
--enable-libusb \
--disable-gssapi \
--enable-acl \
--with-docdir=/usr/share/doc/cups-2.4.12"
